{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "Understanding the Warburg Effect: The Metabolic Requirements of Cell Proliferation", "pmid": null, "doi": "10.1126/science.1160809", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1160809", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1126/science.1160809", "content_for_embedding": "Understanding the Warburg Effect: The Metabolic Requirements of Cell Proliferation\n\nFuel Economy for Growing Cells\nSophisticated 21st-century analyses of the signaling pathways that control cell growth have led researchers back to the seminal work of Otto Warburg, who discovered in the 1920s that tumor cells generate their energy in an unusual way—by switching from mitochondrial respiration to glycolysis. The advantage conferred by this metabolic switch is puzzling because mitochondrial respiration is a more efficient way to produce ATP.\nVander Heiden\net al.\n(p.\n1029\n) review arguments that rapidly growing cells have critical metabolic requirements that extend beyond ATP and that a better understanding of these requirements may shed new light on the “Warburg effect” and ultimately lead to new therapies for cancer.", "abstract": "Fuel Economy for Growing Cells\nSophisticated 21st-century analyses of the signaling pathways that control cell growth have led researchers back to the seminal work of Otto Warburg, who discovered in the 1920s that tumor cells generate their energy in an unusual way—by switching from mitochondrial respiration to glycolysis. The advantage conferred by this metabolic switch is puzzling because mitochondrial respiration is a more efficient way to produce ATP.\nVander Heiden\net al.\n(p.\n1029\n) review arguments that rapidly growing cells have critical metabolic requirements that extend beyond ATP and that a better understanding of these requirements may shed new light on the “Warburg effect” and ultimately lead to new therapies for cancer.", "year": 2009.0, "journal": "Science", "authors": ["Matthew G. Vander Heiden", "Lewis C. Cantley", "Craig B. Thompson"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 68.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Vander Heiden MG, Cantley LC, Thompson CB. Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation. Science324(5930), 1029-1033 (2009). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Vander Heiden MG, Cantley LC, Thompson CB. Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation. Science324(5930), 1029-1033 (2009). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation\"[Title]", "\"Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation\"[Title] AND 2009[dp]", "\"Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation\"[Title/Abstract] AND 2009[dp]", "Vander Heiden MG, Cantley LC, Thompson CB. Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation. Science324(5930), 1029-1033 (2009). Crossref, Medline, CAS AND 2009[dp]", "Vander Heiden MG, Cantley LC, Thompson CB. Understanding the Warburg effect: the metabolic requirements of cell proliferation. Science324(5930), 1029-1033 (2009). Crossref, Medline, CAS"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}}
{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism", "pmid": "21508971", "doi": "10.1038/nrc3038", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/nrc3038", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1038/nrc3038", "content_for_embedding": "Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism\n\nOtto Warburg pioneered quantitative investigations of cancer cell metabolism, as well as photosynthesis and respiration. Warburg and co-workers showed in the 1920s that, under aerobic conditions, tumour tissues metabolize approximately tenfold more glucose to lactate in a given time than normal tissues, a phenomenon known as the Warburg effect. However, this increase in aerobic glycolysis in cancer cells is often erroneously thought to occur instead of mitochondrial respiration and has been misinterpreted as evidence for damage to respiration instead of damage to the regulation of glycolysis. In fact, many cancers exhibit the Warburg effect while retaining mitochondrial respiration. We re-examine Warburg's observations in relation to the current concepts of cancer metabolism as being intimately linked to alterations of mitochondrial DNA, oncogenes and tumour suppressors, and thus readily exploitable for cancer therapy.", "abstract": "Otto Warburg pioneered quantitative investigations of cancer cell metabolism, as well as photosynthesis and respiration. Warburg and co-workers showed in the 1920s that, under aerobic conditions, tumour tissues metabolize approximately tenfold more glucose to lactate in a given time than normal tissues, a phenomenon known as the Warburg effect. However, this increase in aerobic glycolysis in cancer cells is often erroneously thought to occur instead of mitochondrial respiration and has been misinterpreted as evidence for damage to respiration instead of damage to the regulation of glycolysis. In fact, many cancers exhibit the Warburg effect while retaining mitochondrial respiration. We re-examine Warburg's observations in relation to the current concepts of cancer metabolism as being intimately linked to alterations of mitochondrial DNA, oncogenes and tumour suppressors, and thus readily exploitable for cancer therapy.", "year": 2011.0, "journal": "Nature Reviews Cancer", "authors": ["Willem H. Koppenol", "Patricia L. Bounds", "Chi V. Dang"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 63.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Koppenol WH, Bounds PL, Dang CV. Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism. Nat. Rev. Cancer 11(5), 325-337 (2011). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Koppenol WH, Bounds PL, Dang CV. Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism. Nat. Rev. Cancer 11(5), 325-337 (2011). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism\"[Title]", "\"Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism\"[Title] AND 2011[dp]", "\"Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism\"[Title/Abstract] AND 2011[dp]", "Koppenol WH, Bounds PL, Dang CV. Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism. Nat. Rev. Cancer 11(5), 325-337 (2011). Crossref, Medline, CAS AND 2011[dp]", "Koppenol WH, Bounds PL, Dang CV. Otto Warburg's contributions to current concepts of cancer metabolism. Nat. Rev. Cancer 11(5), 325-337 (2011). Crossref, Medline, CAS"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}, "abstract_source": "pubmed", "abstract_fetched_at": "2026-01-13T15:22:01.110988"}
{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation", "pmid": "20189791", "doi": "10.1016/j.coi.2010.01.018", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coi.2010.01.018", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1016/j.coi.2010.01.018", "content_for_embedding": "Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation\n\nWhen naïve or memory T cells encounter foreign antigen along with proper co-stimulation they undergo rapid and extensive clonal expansion. In mammals, this type of proliferation is fairly unique to cells of the adaptive immune system and requires a considerable expenditure of energy and cellular resources. While research has often focused on the roles of cytokines, antigenic signals, and co-stimulation in guiding T cell responses, data indicate that, at a fundamental level, it is cellular metabolism that regulates T cell function and differentiation and therefore influences the final outcome of the adaptive immune response. This review will focus on some earlier fundamental observations regarding T cell bioenergetics and its role in regulating cellular function, as well as recent work that suggests that manipulating the immune response by targeting lymphocyte metabolism could prove useful in treatments against infection and cancer.", "abstract": "When naïve or memory T cells encounter foreign antigen along with proper co-stimulation they undergo rapid and extensive clonal expansion. In mammals, this type of proliferation is fairly unique to cells of the adaptive immune system and requires a considerable expenditure of energy and cellular resources. While research has often focused on the roles of cytokines, antigenic signals, and co-stimulation in guiding T cell responses, data indicate that, at a fundamental level, it is cellular metabolism that regulates T cell function and differentiation and therefore influences the final outcome of the adaptive immune response. This review will focus on some earlier fundamental observations regarding T cell bioenergetics and its role in regulating cellular function, as well as recent work that suggests that manipulating the immune response by targeting lymphocyte metabolism could prove useful in treatments against infection and cancer.", "year": 2010.0, "journal": "Current Opinion in Immunology", "authors": ["Erika L Pearce"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 63.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Pearce EL. Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation. Curr. Opin. Immunol. 22(3), 314-320 2010). Crossref, Medline,CAS", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Pearce EL. Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation. Curr. Opin. Immunol. 22(3), 314-320 2010). Crossref, Medline,CAS", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation\"[Title]", "\"Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation\"[Title] AND 2010[dp]", "\"Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation\"[Title/Abstract] AND 2010[dp]", "Pearce EL. Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation. Curr. Opin. Immunol. 22(3), 314-320 2010). Crossref, Medline,CAS AND 2010[dp]", "Pearce EL. Metabolism in T cell activation and differentiation. Curr. Opin. Immunol. 22(3), 314-320 2010). Crossref, Medline,CAS"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}, "abstract_source": "pubmed", "abstract_fetched_at": "2026-01-13T15:22:03.280972"}
{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "Fueling Immunity: Insights into Metabolism and Lymphocyte Function", "pmid": null, "doi": "10.1126/science.1242454", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1242454", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1126/science.1242454", "content_for_embedding": "Fueling Immunity: Insights into Metabolism and Lymphocyte Function\n\nLymphocyte Metabolism\nLymphocytes are highly dynamic cells, undergoing extensive proliferation upon infection and then reducing in number upon pathogen clearance. Lymphocytes also circulate through many different tissue environments that vary in their nutrient and oxygen availability. Recent studies have revealed changes in metabolic programming that facilitate this dynamic behavior. How these changes occur and the specific effects that they have on lymphocyte function and on the ultimate outcome of an infection are not well understood.\nPearce\net al.\n(\n1242454\n) review recent progress in this area, suggest how parallels might be found in studying the metabolic changes seen in tumor cells, and propose challenges for the future.", "abstract": "Lymphocyte Metabolism\nLymphocytes are highly dynamic cells, undergoing extensive proliferation upon infection and then reducing in number upon pathogen clearance. Lymphocytes also circulate through many different tissue environments that vary in their nutrient and oxygen availability. Recent studies have revealed changes in metabolic programming that facilitate this dynamic behavior. How these changes occur and the specific effects that they have on lymphocyte function and on the ultimate outcome of an infection are not well understood.\nPearce\net al.\n(\n1242454\n) review recent progress in this area, suggest how parallels might be found in studying the metabolic changes seen in tumor cells, and propose challenges for the future.", "year": 2013.0, "journal": "Science", "authors": ["Erika L. Pearce", "Maya C. Poffenberger", "Chih-Hao Chang", "Russell G. Jones"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 68.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Pearce EL, Poffenberger MC, Chang CH, Jones RG. Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function. Science 342(6155), 1242454(2013). Crossref, Medline", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Pearce EL, Poffenberger MC, Chang CH, Jones RG. Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function. Science 342(6155), 1242454(2013). Crossref, Medline", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function\"[Title]", "\"Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function\"[Title] AND 2013[dp]", "\"Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function\"[Title/Abstract] AND 2013[dp]", "Pearce EL, Poffenberger MC, Chang CH, Jones RG. Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function. Science 342(6155), 1242454(2013). Crossref, Medline AND 2013[dp]", "Pearce EL, Poffenberger MC, Chang CH, Jones RG. Fueling immunity: insights into metabolism and lymphocyte function. Science 342(6155), 1242454(2013). Crossref, Medline"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}}
