{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "PURIFICATION OF PHOSPHATE‐DEPENDENT PIG BRAIN GLUTAMINASE", "pmid": null, "doi": "10.1111/j.1471-4159.1973.tb00090.x", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1471-4159.1973.tb00090.x", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1111/j.1471-4159.1973.tb00090.x", "content_for_embedding": "PURIFICATION OF PHOSPHATE‐DEPENDENT PIG BRAIN GLUTAMINASE\n\nAbstract— A procedure for preparing highly purified phosphate‐activated glutaminase (EC 3.5.1.2, L‐glutamine amidohydrolase) from pig brain is described. The main steps consist of extraction with acetone, followed by sodium sulphate fractionation of the solubilized acetone powder. Thereafter, solubilization by dialysis against a buffer containing tris‐HC1, mercaptoethanol, and EDTA, followed by precipitation with phosphate‐borate, is repeated twice. The final preparation contains no impurities which can be detected by polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis, isoelectric focusing, and sedimentation equilibrium centrifugation. By the latter method, molecular weight is determined to be 187,000. By polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis in sodium dodecyl sulphate, one protein band with molecular weight 64,000 is found.", "abstract": "Abstract— A procedure for preparing highly purified phosphate‐activated glutaminase (EC 3.5.1.2, L‐glutamine amidohydrolase) from pig brain is described. The main steps consist of extraction with acetone, followed by sodium sulphate fractionation of the solubilized acetone powder. Thereafter, solubilization by dialysis against a buffer containing tris‐HC1, mercaptoethanol, and EDTA, followed by precipitation with phosphate‐borate, is repeated twice. The final preparation contains no impurities which can be detected by polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis, isoelectric focusing, and sedimentation equilibrium centrifugation. By the latter method, molecular weight is determined to be 187,000. By polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis in sodium dodecyl sulphate, one protein band with molecular weight 64,000 is found.", "year": 1973.0, "journal": "Journal of Neurochemistry", "authors": ["G. Svenneby", "I. Aa. Torgner", "E. Kvamme"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 68.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Svenneby G, Torgner IA, Kvamme E. Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase. J. Neurochem. 20(4), 1217-1224 (1973). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Svenneby G, Torgner IA, Kvamme E. Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase. J. Neurochem. 20(4), 1217-1224 (1973). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase\"[Title]", "\"Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase\"[Title] AND 1973[dp]", "\"Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase\"[Title/Abstract] AND 1973[dp]", "Svenneby G, Torgner IA, Kvamme E. Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase. J. Neurochem. 20(4), 1217-1224 (1973). Crossref, Medline, CAS AND 1973[dp]", "Svenneby G, Torgner IA, Kvamme E. Purification of phosphate-dependent pig brain glutaminase. J. Neurochem. 20(4), 1217-1224 (1973). Crossref, Medline, CAS"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}}
{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis", "pmid": null, "doi": "10.1016/0003-9861(76)90326-x", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-9861(76)90326-x", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1016/0003-9861(76)90326-x", "content_for_embedding": "Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis", "abstract": null, "year": 1976.0, "journal": "Archives of Biochemistry and Biophysics", "authors": ["Norman P. Curthoys", "Theresa Kuhlenschmidt", "Susan S. Godfrey"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 63.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Curthoys NP, Kuhlenschmidt T, Godfrey SS. Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis. Arch. Biochem. Biophys. 174(1), 82-89 (1976). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Curthoys NP, Kuhlenschmidt T, Godfrey SS. Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis. Arch. Biochem. Biophys. 174(1), 82-89 (1976). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis\"[Title]", "\"Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis\"[Title] AND 1976[dp]", "\"Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis\"[Title/Abstract] AND 1976[dp]", "Curthoys NP, Kuhlenschmidt T, Godfrey SS. Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis. Arch. Biochem. Biophys. 174(1), 82-89 (1976). Crossref, Medline, CAS AND 1976[dp]", "Curthoys NP, Kuhlenschmidt T, Godfrey SS. Regulation of renal ammoniagenesis. Arch. Biochem. Biophys. 174(1), 82-89 (1976). Crossref, Medline, CAS"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}}
{"source_type": "crossref", "title": "Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney", "pmid": null, "doi": "10.1042/bj2290399", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1042/bj2290399", "claim_ids": [], "tags": ["crossref", "resolved_via_crossref", "resolved_from_unresolved"], "id": "src_doi_10.1042/bj2290399", "content_for_embedding": "Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney\n\nA phosphate-dependent glutaminase was purified 1200-fold from rat brain. In the absence of a polyvalent anion, the glutaminase exists as an inactive protomer which has an estimated Mr of 126000. The addition of 100mM-phosphate causes maximal activation and a dimerization (Mr 249000) of the glutaminase. The phosphate activation is sigmoidal, with a K0.5 of 25mM and a Hill coefficient (h) of 1.5 Glutamate inhibition is competitive with respect to glutamine and is decreased by increasing the concentration of phosphate. Phosphate also decreases the Km for glutamine. The purified glutaminase contains a predominant peptide (Mr 65000) and a minor peptide (Mr 68000) that are present in an approximate ratio of 4:1 respectively. The glutaminase immunoprecipitated from freshly solubilized brain tissue or from synaptosomal and non-synaptosomal brain mitochondria contains the same distribution of the two peptides. In contrast, the glutaminase purified from rat kidney contains five to seven peptides that range in Mr value from 59000 to 48000, and immunoprecipitates derived from freshly solubilized renal tissue contain only the Mr-65000 peptide. Partial proteolysis and size fractionation of the three immunoprecipitated peptides indicate that they are structurally related. The series of peptides characteristic of the purified renal glutaminase is generated on storage of the solubilized extract of kidney tissue. The glutaminase contained in the solubilized brain extract is not degraded unless a renal extract is added. Thus the difference in the pattern of peptides associated with the two purified enzymes is due to an endogenous renal proteinase that is not present in brain.", "abstract": "A phosphate-dependent glutaminase was purified 1200-fold from rat brain. In the absence of a polyvalent anion, the glutaminase exists as an inactive protomer which has an estimated Mr of 126000. The addition of 100mM-phosphate causes maximal activation and a dimerization (Mr 249000) of the glutaminase. The phosphate activation is sigmoidal, with a K0.5 of 25mM and a Hill coefficient (h) of 1.5 Glutamate inhibition is competitive with respect to glutamine and is decreased by increasing the concentration of phosphate. Phosphate also decreases the Km for glutamine. The purified glutaminase contains a predominant peptide (Mr 65000) and a minor peptide (Mr 68000) that are present in an approximate ratio of 4:1 respectively. The glutaminase immunoprecipitated from freshly solubilized brain tissue or from synaptosomal and non-synaptosomal brain mitochondria contains the same distribution of the two peptides. In contrast, the glutaminase purified from rat kidney contains five to seven peptides that range in Mr value from 59000 to 48000, and immunoprecipitates derived from freshly solubilized renal tissue contain only the Mr-65000 peptide. Partial proteolysis and size fractionation of the three immunoprecipitated peptides indicate that they are structurally related. The series of peptides characteristic of the purified renal glutaminase is generated on storage of the solubilized extract of kidney tissue. The glutaminase contained in the solubilized brain extract is not degraded unless a renal extract is added. Thus the difference in the pattern of peptides associated with the two purified enzymes is due to an endogenous renal proteinase that is not present in brain.", "year": 1985.0, "journal": "Biochemical Journal", "authors": ["W G Haser", "R A Shapiro", "N P Curthoys"], "original": null, "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0.0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 68.0, "match_similarity": null, "original_citation_line": null, "search_query_used": null, "search_queries_tried": null, "low_confidence_match": null, "original_unresolved": {"source_type": "unresolved_citation", "title": "Haser WG, Shapiro RA, Curthoys NP. Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem. J. 229(2), 399-408 (1985). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "url": null, "pmid": null, "doi": null, "abstract": null, "journal": null, "year": null, "authors": [], "pub_types": [], "evidence_score_0_5": 0, "evidence_strength": "unknown", "tags": ["unresolved"], "original_citation_line": "Haser WG, Shapiro RA, Curthoys NP. Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem. J. 229(2), 399-408 (1985). Crossref, Medline, CAS", "search_queries_tried": ["\"Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem\"[Title]", "\"Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem\"[Title/Abstract]", "\"Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem\"[Title] AND 1985[dp]", "\"Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem\"[Title/Abstract] AND 1985[dp]", "Haser WG, Shapiro RA, Curthoys NP. Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem. J. 229(2), 399-408 (1985). Crossref, Medline, CAS AND 1985[dp]", "Haser WG, Shapiro RA, Curthoys NP. Comparison of the phosphate-dependent glutaminase obtained from rat brain and kidney: Biochem. J. 229(2), 399-408 (1985). Crossref, Medline, CAS"], "chatbot_data_quality_score_0_100": 17}}
